Pages = {232-257},
Month = {MAR},
Abstract = {The advent of lidar has revolutionized the way we observe and measure
   vegetation structure from the ground and from above and represents a
   major advance toward the quantification of 3D ecological observations.
   Developments in lidar hardware systems and data processing algorithms
   have greatly improved the accessibility and ease of use of lidar
   observations in ecological studies. A wide range of studies has been
   devoted to accurately measuring and modeling vegetation structural and
   functional attributes from lidar data across a range of spatial scales
   (from individual organs to global scales) and ecosystem types (e.g.,
   forest, agricultural, grassland, and urban ecosystems).},
